## 10. Database Persistence

**Every website edit that should survive a refresh must be written to the database or Storage.** Updating only the visible HTML, JavaScript variables, or temporary component state is not sufficient.

### 10.1 Read records

```javascript
async function getPatientRecords(patientId) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("YOUR_MEDICAL_RECORDS_TABLE")
    .select("*")
    .eq("patient_id", patientId);

  if (error) {
    console.error("Could not load records:", error.message);
    return null;
  }

  return data;
}
```

Use the actual table and column names from your existing schema.

### 10.2 Insert a new record

```javascript
async function createRecord(record) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("YOUR_EXISTING_TABLE")
    .insert(record)
    .select();

  if (error) {
    console.error("Could not save record:", error.message);
    return null;
  }

  return data;
}
```

### 10.3 Update an existing record

```javascript
async function updateRecord(recordId, updates) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("YOUR_EXISTING_TABLE")
    .update(updates)
    .eq("id", recordId)
    .select();

  if (error) {
    console.error("Update failed:", error.message);
    return null;
  }

  if (!data || data.length === 0) {
    console.warn("No record was updated.");
    return null;
  }

  return data[0];
}
```

The record ID must be the real identifier of the row being edited. The database's RLS policies must authorize the operation.

### 10.4 Delete a record

```javascript
async function deleteRecord(recordId) {
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("YOUR_EXISTING_TABLE")
    .delete()
    .eq("id", recordId)
    .select();

  if (error) {
    console.error("Delete failed:", error.message);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.isArray(data) && data.length > 0;
}
```

Deletion should only be implemented where appropriate. Medical records may need retention rules or a documented correction process instead of ordinary deletion.

### 10.5 Connect a form to a database update

Example pattern for an existing patient edit form:

```javascript
const patientForm = document.querySelector("#patientForm");

patientForm?.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();

  const patientId = patientForm.dataset.patientId;

  if (!patientId) {
    alert("Patient ID is missing.");
    return;
  }

  const formData = new FormData(patientForm);

  const updates = {
    full_name: String(formData.get("full_name") || "").trim()
  };

  const savedPatient = await updateRecord(
    patientId,
    updates
  );

  if (savedPatient) {
    alert("Changes saved successfully.");
    // Reload the relevant patient information here.
  } else {
    alert("Unable to save changes. Check permissions and logs.");
  }
});
```

This is an illustrative pattern. Match the form selector, table, field names, and primary key to the actual application.

### 10.6 Refresh the interface after saving

After a successful insert or update:

1. Use the returned database record where appropriate.
2. Refresh the relevant table or profile view.
3. Show a success message only after the operation succeeds.
4. Show a useful error message if the operation fails.
5. Avoid reporting success when the database request was rejected.

### 10.7 RLS policies

Row Level Security must be enabled and correctly configured for tables containing patient information.

Policies should define which authenticated users may:

- Read a particular record.
- Insert a record for an authorized patient.
- Update a record they are allowed to manage.
- Delete a record, where deletion is permitted.

Do not use an unrestricted public policy as a shortcut. Avoid disabling RLS to make a failing update work.

### 10.8 Persistence verification

To verify a change:

1. Sign in to the Curex website.
2. Edit an authorized demo record.
3. Click Save.
4. Wait for the database request to finish.
5. Refresh the page.
6. Confirm the edited value is still displayed.
7. Open Supabase Table Editor and verify the stored row, if your account has permission to view it.

If the change disappears after refreshing, check whether the form actually sends a database request and whether the page reloads its data from Supabase.

